{
  // ⚠️ TEST CONFIG. `wrangler dev` ONLY — never `wrangler deploy`.
  //
  // The e2e "worker lane" runs the suite against the thing we actually ship:
  // the OpenNext bundle, executed by workerd. `npm run e2e:server:worker`
  // builds it and starts `wrangler dev` with this file (see docs/E2E.md).
  //
  // It is a copy of wrangler.jsonc with exactly three deliberate differences —
  // each of which is also the reason it must never reach production:
  //
  //  1. NO `routes`. The deployed config claims the custom domain
  //     tictactoe.sundaysuite.app. A dev server has no business owning that
  //     name, and `wrangler deploy -c wrangler.e2e.jsonc` would otherwise fight
  //     the real Worker for it.
  //  2. NO `global_fetch_strictly_public`. That flag makes every subrequest go
  //     out over the public internet, so the Worker could not reach the local
  //     Supabase the suite boots on 127.0.0.1:54321 — the requests would be
  //     refused, and every server route would fail. Production keeps the flag:
  //     it is what stops a Worker from being used to probe private addresses.
  //  3. `observability` OFF. A dev server's logs go to the terminal; there is
  //     no account to bill or dashboard to fill.
  //
  // Everything the runtime SEMANTICS depend on is unchanged on purpose —
  // `compatibility_date` and `nodejs_compat` are exactly the deployed values,
  // because a lane that ran under a different compat date would be testing a
  // runtime nobody ships.
  //
  // The name differs too (`-e2e` suffix): a stray `wrangler deploy` with this
  // file creates a separate, unrouted Worker rather than overwriting the live
  // one.
  //
  // ⚠️ E2E_SEAM: the lane passes `--var E2E_SEAM:1` on the COMMAND LINE, never
  // from this file. The seam (`POST /api/dev/quickmatch`, unauthenticated
  // tournament/player/game minting) must not be reachable through anything that
  // is committed — see docs/E2E.md.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "sundaytictactoe-e2e",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": false
  }
  // Runtime env for the lane comes from `.dev.vars` (gitignored; written by the
  // CI job from `supabase status -o env`, written by hand locally):
  //   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY,
  //   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL
  // The NEXT_PUBLIC_* pair is ALSO needed at build time (Next inlines it into
  // the client bundle); .dev.vars covers the server half.
}
